id: decisions/009-audience-primacy
title: "Human-readable first is about the record, proof goes where effects are"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'audience-primacy'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: audience-primacy
axis_gloss: "which reader the artifact is shaped for when the two conflict: a person reading it, or a parser consuming it"
poles:
  a: machine-addressability-first
  b: human-readability-first
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: full
leverage: 24
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(20) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Is 'human-readable first, machine-addressable second' a rule about how a .yawn
  reads and what leads it, or also about where proof effort and currency go —
  and if only the first, does any record say so?

why_it_matters: >
  The first principle in core/design-principles.yawn says human-readable first,
  yet the root file opens with a runtime identity block before its summary, the
  machine selector is 218 lines against 33 for the human entry path, the
  contracts reached v1 while the unified human document is not-yet-specified,
  and the one human onboarding record is two months stale. Once answered, every
  child Yawn below Dave knows whether its human summary must lead and stay
  current with its contracts, or whether the contracts may run ahead so long as
  the record still reads.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    8 judgments sit at machine-addressability-first (0 authored) and 12 at
    human-readability-first (7 authored). The repository asserts both directions
    on this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave meant the first principle as a rule about text or about effort;
    and which human record (readme.yawn, start/, or the unspecified unified human
    document) is the one the contracts must point at.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Machine-addressable first. core/design-principles.yawn reorders its
      first two principles; the root file may lead with official_runtime and
      core_loop refs; the human onboarding path is a derived view refreshed when
      contracts change; normative lists are encoded as YAML an agent can read, with
      prose as commentary.
    cost: "Strikes an authored first principle repeated in four records (design-principles, yawn-spine-dogfood, orientation-shape, value-proposition); the yawn.ai public voice rule and start/ folder lose their basis."
    risk: "The protocol becomes another schema project; the leading question in references/july-01-lock.yawn (how a human moves from implicit pattern to explicit agency) is no longer what the tree is for."
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "None new; strike the principle and let the existing schema tests stand as the only proof."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0186"
      - "J0238"
      - "J0243"
      - "J0244"
      - "J0769"
      - "J1036"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Human-readable first for both text and effort. The root file leads with
      summary; every machine contract that reaches v1 has a human record of equal
      currency updated in the same change; core/human-use.yawn may not be older than
      the contracts it explains; boundaries and failure tests are encoded in the
      same form as invariants so a person and an agent read the same list.
    cost: "Reorder yawn.yawn; rewrite or refresh core/human-use.yawn and the unified human document before any further contract advances; encode section 17 and 18 of RELATIONSHIP_FIRST_AGENT_ARENA as YAML; a currency check that ties every schema change to a prose change."
    risk: "Currency-by-timestamp is gamed by touching updated: without changing content; human records get edited for a test rather than for a reader."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "tests/human-currency.test.mjs: yawn.yawn's first top-level key after id/title/kind/status/updated is summary:; for every schemas/*.schema.json at v1 named by a core/ or interface/ record, that record's updated: is not older than the schema's last git change; core/human-use.yawn updated: is not older than core/inquiry-selection.yawn."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0201"
      - "J0377"
      - "J0554"
      - "J0559"
      - "J0690"
      - "J0330"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Human-readable first governs the record: a human sentence leads every
      .yawn (summary before runtime identity), normative lists read the same way for
      a person and an agent, and a machine block never replaces the human one.
      carve-out, written down: proof effort goes where effects are — the machine
      layer may run ahead in validators, schemas and tests, stated as 'proof-bound
      when promoted' (examples/yawn-spine-dogfood.yawn). One obligation ties the
      layers: a contract that reaches v1 names the human record that explains it,
      and that record is not older than the contract.
    cost: "Move summary above official_runtime in yawn.yawn; add a `explained_by:` pointer to each v1 schema's index entry in schemas/node.yawn; refresh core/human-use.yawn once; write the carve-out into core/design-principles.yawn as a third line."
    risk: "The carve-out becomes the rule in practice and the human layer stays the one that is always 'next'; the explained_by pointer resolves to a stale record."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend scripts/validate-hub-links.mjs: every v1 schema listed in schemas/node.yawn holds carries explained_by resolving to a core/ or interface/ record whose updated: is not older than the schema's last git change; a test that yawn.yawn leads with summary; keep the existing contract tests as the machine side's proof."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0201"
      - "J0377"
      - "J0554"
      - "J0238"
      - "J0769"
      - "J1036"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.65
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    The authored side is unanimous on the principle (seven authored judgments,
    none against) but J0201 gives no reason and the six REVEALED judgments show
    where the repository actually spends: validators, schemas and tests all sit on
    the machine side, the human entry record is the oldest file in core/, and the
    manifest's own file_priority is contradicted by its own key order.
    GROUND_TRUTH fact 9 corroborates: where the repo bought proof it paid in
    human-readability. The reconciling formula is already authored — J0377's
    'human-readable first, machine-addressable second, proof-bound when promoted'
    — and C is that formula written as a rule with one obligation that stops the
    human layer from being permanently 'next'. B is what the authored records
    literally say and would be right if Dave means effort, not text; A contradicts
    four authored records. Close between B and C; C is what the passing suite
    already implements plus one link.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "yawn.yawn: summary: is the first top-level key after the header, ahead of official_runtime and core_loop refs."
    - "Every v1 schema listed in schemas/node.yawn names the human record that explains it, and scripts/validate-hub-links.mjs resolves the pointer."
    - "core/human-use.yawn is refreshed and not older than core/inquiry-selection.yawn, and a test in tests/ fails if that inverts again."
    - "core/design-principles.yawn carries the carve-out as a stated line, so 'human-readable first' is no longer contradicted by where the proofs sit."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    yawn.yawn still leads with official_runtime, core/human-use.yawn stays at
    2026-06-30 while contracts advance to v1, and nothing in npm test notices.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0186"
  - "J0238"
  - "J0243"
  - "J0244"
  - "J0769"
  - "J1036"
  - "J0201"
  - "J0330"
  - "J0377"
  - "J0554"
  - "J0559"
  - "J0690"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
